# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored.
#
# NOTE ON THE FOUNDRY RESOURCE: this app currently points at the SAME Foundry
# resource as ../Travel-Assistant. That is fine for a POC and it is written
# down here on purpose rather than silently inherited — a second app quietly
# sharing a first app's Azure resource is exactly the class of surprise
# FDE.md Pillar 8 records (a Container Apps revision serving a stale image
# because nobody wrote down which tag it referenced).
#
# Consequence to know: the embedding cache and vector index for this app are
# LOCAL files (evals/), but the embedding calls bill to the travel resource.
# Give this app its own resource before any cost number is quoted to anyone.

# OpenAI-compatible endpoint on the Foundry resource.
# Foundry portal > project > Overview.
#
# HOST SUFFIX GOTCHA: this is `.services.ai.azure.com`, NOT `.openai.azure.com`.
# Both hostnames exist for a Foundry resource and only one works with the
# `/openai/v1` path used by this client. The wrong one fails with a 404 that
# reads like a bad deployment name, which is a good way to lose an hour.
FOUNDRY_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=https://<resource>.services.ai.azure.com/openai/v1

# Deployment names — Foundry portal > Build > Deployments.
# These are the DEPLOYMENT names chosen in the portal, not model ids.
FOUNDRY_CHAT_DEPLOYMENT=
FOUNDRY_EMBEDDING_DEPLOYMENT=

# No keys. Auth is DefaultAzureCredential: `az login` locally, managed identity
# in Azure, same code path either way. If you find yourself adding a key here,
# stop and read FDE.md Pillar 6.

# Postgres with the pgvector extension — where the policy index lives.
# UNSET = the local Docker container below, which is what the README starts.
#
# This is the ONE line that changes per engagement. Neon, RDS, Azure Database
# for PostgreSQL, or the customer's own cluster inside their own network — all
# just a connection string. That portability is the reason pgvector was chosen
# over a managed search service: "can we run Postgres" is never a blocker.
#
#   docker run -d --name claims-pgvector \
#     -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres -e POSTGRES_USER=postgres -e POSTGRES_DB=postgres \
#     -p 5433:5432 pgvector/pgvector:pg17
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5433/postgres

# Shared secret for the HTTP API, once one exists.
# UNSET = loopback-only (the safe default for local dev)
# SET   = required as the x-api-key header, from any address
# The guard must fail CLOSED: a missing value reduces access, never grants it.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Langfuse — pillar 4's dashboard and run history.
#
# ALL THREE OR NONE. Unset, `pnpm eval` behaves exactly as it always has and no
# telemetry is produced: the eval suite must never need a running service to
# produce a number. Half-set is treated as a misconfiguration and throws at
# startup, rather than silently recording nothing for twenty minutes.
#
# Start the server:  docker compose -f docker-compose.langfuse.yml up -d
# Then open http://localhost:3000, create a project, and copy its two keys.
#
# BASE_URL IS THE DATA-RESIDENCY CONTROL. Traces carry policy ids, coverage
# amounts and the model's reasoning about a named customer's entitlements.
# Point this at your own server, not at a vendor's cloud — see the header of
# docker-compose.langfuse.yml for why that distinction is a procurement
# question and not just a preference.
LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=

# Which embedding model. foundry (default) = text-embedding-3-small on Azure.
# local = a small model on this machine: no credential, no cost, 384 dims.
# Vectors from the two are NOT comparable — changing this means re-ingesting.
EMBEDDINGS=foundry

# --- Meridian Pharma (second domain, docs/PHARMA-PLAN.md) ---------------------
# Base URL for the pharma estate. Six databases (mrd_erp, mrd_mes, mrd_hcm,
# mrd_qms, mrd_tms, mrd_reg) are derived from it by swapping the database name.
# Keep this on a DIFFERENT project from DATABASE_URL so the two domains cannot
# reach each other's tables.
PHARMA_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
# The base URL for the four Vantis steering databases (vst_crm, vst_plm, vst_alm,
# vst_pmo). The database name in the path is ignored — each is derived by
# swapping it. The code base is NOT a database: it is files under
# docs/steering/corpus/, written by `pnpm steering:corpus`.
STEERING_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# --- Thornbury Goods (fifth engagement, docs/commerce/PLAN.md) ---------------
# Base URL for the Thornbury estate. FIVE databases (thb_shop, thb_wms,
# thb_fleet, thb_crm, thb_policy) are derived from it by swapping the database
# name in the path, so the name here is only the admin connection.
# Note the variable name: ECOMMERCE_DB_URL, not COMMERCE_DATABASE_URL.
# Keep it on its OWN Neon project. `pnpm commerce:env-check` refuses a URL that
# names another engagement's database (mrd_*, vst_*) or shares a host with one,
# because a mistyped base URL is the same typo that later drops an estate.
ECOMMERCE_DB_URL=postgresql://user:password@host.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
# CREATE/DROP ONLY, and OPTIONAL. CREATE DATABASE is not transactional and
# pgbouncer refuses it, with an error about transaction blocks that never
# mentions poolers -- so it has to run against the direct, non-pooled endpoint.
# The code DERIVES that by stripping `-pooler` from the host, which is correct
# on Neon (verified 2026-09-18). Set this only if your provider's direct host
# is not its pooled host minus the suffix. Never point the API at it.
ECOMMERCE_DB_DIRECT_URL=postgresql://user:password@host.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# ── RUNNING IT FOR FREE, ON THIS MACHINE ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing below needs a cloud account. Measured on an RTX 3090 (24 GB) on
# 2026-09-16; `pnpm local:check` is the producer for the claims in this block.
#
#   LOOP=mastra LLM_PROVIDER=local EMBEDDINGS=local pnpm ask "…"
#
# LEFT COMMENTED OUT DELIBERATELY. This file is the template for `cp
# .env.example .env`, and SILENCE MEANS AZURE is a property
# `pnpm provider:check` asserts. Uncommenting these would make a fresh clone
# default to a server that may not be running — which fails as a connection
# error and reads like a broken install.
#
# THE ENGINE MATTERS AND IT IS NOT A PREFERENCE. `LOOP` defaults to `sdk`, which
# drives the Responses API; Ollama and llama.cpp serve /v1/chat/completions and
# do not implement /v1/responses. The sdk engine therefore REFUSES local rather
# than failing at the transport with an error that reads like a broken install.
# `mastra` and `langgraph` both serve it. See docs/ENGINES.md §3.
# LLM_PROVIDER=local
# LOCAL_OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
# A tag Ollama has pulled — NOT the Azure deployment name. Passing `gpt-5-mini`
# here fails with "model not found", which reads like a missing pull.
# LOCAL_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b
#
# AVOID A REASONING MODEL FOR THE TOOL LOOP. `qwen3:8b` passes every structured
# output and tool-calling check, and then spends thinking tokens before EVERY
# tool call — in a 12-turn loop that is the difference between a demo and a
# timeout. `qwen2.5:7b` passes the same checks with no thinking phase.
#
# EMBEDDINGS ARE A SEPARATE SWITCH, and switching means RE-INGESTING: local is
# bge-small at 384 dimensions against text-embedding-3-small's 1536. The two
# cannot share a table, so `DOMAIN.vectorTable` appends `_local` — the hosted
# index is left intact and the two can never silently mix.
# EMBEDDINGS=local

# ── SOMEONE ELSE'S GPU, ON A FREE TIER ────────────────────────────────
#
# A SEPARATE VALUE FROM `local`, AND NOT A TIDIER SPELLING OF IT. The two share
# a builder and differ in the only thing that matters:
#
#   local    loopback, no credential, nothing leaves the machine
#   hosted   a third party, a real API key, and YOUR PROMPTS LEAVE
#
# Collapsing them would let `LLM_PROVIDER=local` mean "Google". A variable whose
# value no longer describes where the data went is worse than no variable.
#
# FREE TIERS COMMONLY TRAIN ON WHAT YOU SEND. Harmless here — `docs/examples/`
# is fabricated — and disqualifying at a real engagement. That is the whole
# subject of docs/beyond-retrieval/CREDENTIALS.md.
#
# The default base URL is Gemini's OpenAI-compatible surface, because its free
# tier serves the three things this repo's contract needs on one endpoint:
# /chat/completions, tool calling, and a strict json_schema. Key from
# https://aistudio.google.com/apikey — no card. Groq, OpenRouter, Together and
# a remote vLLM all work by setting HOSTED_BASE_URL.
#
# THERE IS NO DEFAULT MODEL ON PURPOSE. A hosted model id is a product name that
# gets retired; baking one in makes this file a 404 on a date nobody chose.
# Verify the endpoint before trusting it:  LLM_PROVIDER=hosted pnpm compat:check
# LOOP=mastra MUST BE SET ALONGSIDE THIS. Omit it and every command fails
# instantly with "cannot be served by the agents-sdk engine" — LOOP defaults to
# `sdk`, which drives the Responses API, and no OpenAI-compatible provider
# implements it. Eight eval cases failed at 0.0s to prove it.
# LOOP=mastra
# LLM_PROVIDER=hosted
# HOSTED_API_KEY=
# HOSTED_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-16, and the lesson is that AVAILABILITY BEATS CAPABILITY on a
# free tier: gemini-3.6/3.7/3.8-flash all returned 503 "high demand", while
# gemini-2.5-flash returned 404 because it had been retired. The `-lite` variant
# answered in 731ms against 12s for the full flash, and got the hard eval case
# (cov-008) right. Pick for availability first. See docs/FREE.md §8.
# HOSTED_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/openai
